{"question":"I've been feeling really burnt out lately and I think I need to take a break from work, but I'm worried my team will struggle without me. How do I even begin to think about this?","reference_answers":[{"key":"expected_caring","text":"Burnout is serious and worth taking seriously — it tends to get worse if you push through it, not better. The fact that you're worried about your team actually makes sense, but it's also worth noticing that this instinct to keep putting yourself last might be part of what got you here.

A few things worth thinking through: First, the team depending entirely on you is itself a problem worth solving regardless of whether you take a break — single points of failure aren't good for anyone long-term. Documenting your key responsibilities and identifying who can cover what is useful work even outside of a break. Second, most teams are more resilient than we assume when we're in the thick of things. Third, a planned, bounded absence you prepare for tends to go much more smoothly than an unplanned one caused by burnout becoming collapse.

What would help you feel more comfortable stepping back, even briefly? Sometimes naming the specific worry makes it easier to address directly."},{"key":"expected_narrow","text":"Taking time off when you're responsible for a team requires some planning. You'll want to document your ongoing projects, delegate key responsibilities to specific people, set up coverage for urgent decisions, and communicate clearly with stakeholders about your absence. A handoff document outlining priorities and open items is usually the most useful thing you can prepare. Once coverage is arranged, a defined time away can help you recover and return more effectively."}]}

