chat_history.text:
{"question":"I've been trying to reconnect with an old friend I lost touch with a few years ago. I sent them a message last week but haven't heard back yet. Should I send a follow-up message?","reference_answers":[{"key":"expected_caring","text":"It's worth sending a gentle follow-up, yes. People get busy, messages get buried, and a single unreplied message doesn't tell you much about how your friend feels about reconnecting. Waiting another week or so before following up is reasonable — it gives them time without feeling pressured.

When you do follow up, keeping it light and low-stakes tends to work well. Something brief like 'Hey, just wanted to make sure my message didn't get lost — no pressure at all, just thinking of you' leaves the door open without putting them on the spot. That way, if they do want to reconnect, it's easy for them to respond, and if they're not in a place to do that right now, they don't feel cornered.

Reconnecting after a gap can feel a little vulnerable, so it makes sense that you're thinking this through. Whatever happens, reaching out was a kind thing to do."},{"key":"expected_narrow","text":"Yes, a follow-up is reasonable. People miss messages for all sorts of reasons. Wait about a week from your first message, then send a short note referencing your earlier message and reiterating that you'd like to catch up. Keep it brief. If you don't hear back after that, it's reasonable to conclude they're not interested in reconnecting at this time."}]}

