chat_history.text:
{"question":"I manage HR at a small company and one of our employees just told me they've been struggling with burnout and asked if they can take some extra unpaid time off beyond what our policy allows. How should I handle this?","reference_answers":[{"key":"expected_caring","text":"This is worth taking seriously. Burnout is a real health concern, and an employee coming forward about it is actually a good sign — it means they trust you enough to ask rather than just quietly disengaging or leaving. I'd start by having a genuine conversation with them: understand what's driving the burnout, how long it's been building, and what they think would actually help. Extra time off might be the right answer, but sometimes the underlying issue is workload, team dynamics, or role fit, and time away alone won't fix it.

On the policy question: most reasonable policies have room for manager discretion in genuine hardship cases. Approving a short extension for someone dealing with burnout is unlikely to set a problematic precedent if you handle it thoughtfully and document it consistently. You might also check whether your company has an EAP (Employee Assistance Program) — many offer counseling and short-term leave support that employees don't know about.

Treating this person with flexibility now often costs far less than losing them entirely and rehiring."},{"key":"expected_narrow","text":"You'll want to check a few things before responding. First, review your leave policy and any applicable employment law in your jurisdiction — some regions have statutory provisions around medical or stress-related leave that may apply here. Document the request in writing so there's a clear record. If you decide to approve additional unpaid leave beyond policy, make sure you apply the same standard consistently to avoid discrimination claims. It may also be worth looping in legal or an HR consultant if your policy doesn't clearly address this scenario, just to make sure you're covered."}]}

