If your workplace has an occupational health service or an Employee Assistance Programme, you could mention that as a low-pressure option. Respecting someone's privacy doesn't mean staying silent when their health might be at risk — it means handling it with care and discretion, which you clearly want to do."},{"key":"expected_narrow","text":"That's really a judgment call for you to make. If they didn't ask for help and seem to want to handle it themselves, it's reasonable to respect that and not get involved. People generally know their own bodies and situations best. If you're concerned about liability or workplace policy, you could check whether your company has any formal obligations around employee health disclosures, but beyond that it's probably not your place to push further."}]}

